KG42.register("rules-6d4190d44739de3cc48b54ff",{"id":"49-residential_shared_storage_market::v141-c-R005","paper":"49-residential_shared_storage_market","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R005","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Counterparty matching","process_stage":"matching","market_meaning":"The aggregator is responsible for matching P2P energy transactions. Scope: The proposed local P2P energy trading market embedded with residential shared energy storage. Buyers’ selection of sellers is conditional on relative P2P transaction prices: buyers choose lower-priced sellers and turn to other sellers if a seller raises its price. Scope: The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. Condition: Total PV generation exceeds buyers’ total energy demand; buyers compare sellers’ P2P transaction prices and switch away from a seller who raises its price when lower-priced sellers are available.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function matching.counterparties under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.local_operator","matching.counterparties","pricing.local"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R005-E001","source":"coordination.local_operator","target":"matching.counterparties","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e003"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R005-E002","source":"matching.counterparties","target":"pricing.local","type":"conditional_on","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g03-e15"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. | The proposed local P2P energy trading market embedded with residential shared energy storage.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e003","v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The aggregator is responsible for matching P2P energy transactions. Scope: The proposed local P2P energy trading market embedded with residential shared energy storage.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e003"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Buyers’ selection of sellers is conditional on relative P2P transaction prices: buyers choose lower-priced sellers and turn to other sellers if a seller raises its price. Scope: The illustrated t = 15 surplus-supply period, including the hypothetical seller-price increase used to explain buyers’ counterparty choices. Condition: Total PV generation exceeds buyers’ total energy demand; buyers compare sellers’ P2P transaction prices and switch away from a seller who raises its price when lower-priced sellers are available.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g03-e15"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e003","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2 System configuration","anchor_text":"ng result. The solid arrows present the P2P transaction between typical ir energy sellers and energy","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The P2P market has an aggregator, who takes the responsibility of matching the P2P energy transaction and operating the SES."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"market equilibrium problem. Table 4 provides a direct insight into how ac market equilibrium arises.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"When t = 15, the total PV generations exceed the total energy demand of buyers, which means the renewable energy generations can not only satisfy all P2P transaction demand but also have to sell excess energy to gird. Given the context, buyers will trade with sellers who require a lower price. And sellers have the motivation to decrease their price in exchange for competitiveness in P2P transactions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-4c60176b12-p10-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2.2 Peer-to-peer market equilibrium","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 308 (2022) 118400 price in exchange for competitiveness in P2P transactions. A conspi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"A conspicuous feature is that each buyer purchases energy from different sellers with the same price 0.1350. The reason is that if a seller improves the P2P transaction price, buyers will not buy energy from him and turn to other sellers with lower prices, causing a revenue drop to the aforementioned seller. Besides, the same price is the FiT plus the transaction cost λ^{cost}. FiT can be seen as the marginal revenue of selling energy to the grid, which is identical to all sellers. The same parameter setting for all sellers accounts for the same P2P transaction price."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":"No decision or selection variable is defined in the supplied matching-rule evidence. C001 states an institutional responsibility, and C002 narrates buyer behavior in the t = 15 case without defining a formal counterparty-selection variable, its domain, or an optimization role.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed coefficient, threshold, or calibrated matching parameter is defined in the supplied matching-rule evidence. Relative P2P prices and the illustrative 0.1350 value are discussed qualitatively in the case example; they are not presented as matching-rule parameters with specified values or domains in this scope."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e003","quote":"The P2P market has an aggregator, who takes the responsibility of matching the P2P energy transaction and operating the SES."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","quote":"When t = 15, the total PV generations exceed the total energy demand of buyers, which means the renewable energy generations can not only satisfy all P2P transaction demand but also have to sell excess energy to gird. Given the context, buyers will trade with sellers who require a lower price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e15","quote":"The reason is that if a seller improves the P2P transaction price, buyers will not buy energy from him and turn to other sellers with lower prices, causing a revenue drop to the aforementioned seller."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"5e7882e1c864a76c94f071d1fd1fe0f18ae6e5c534cddb98158103f10e30da42","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen rule contains (C001) the aggregator's institutional responsibility for matching P2P transactions and (C002) a qualitative conditional from the t = 15 surplus-supply case: buyers favor lower-priced sellers and switch away from a seller that raises its price when lower-priced alternatives exist. The supplied source evidence gives no matching equation, no counterparty-selection variable, no quantifier/domain structure, and no calibrated matching parameter. The numerical price 0.1350 is an illustrative case observation, not a formal rule parameter. The rule is therefore retained as a non-mathematical procedural/logical description rather than forced into an unsupported formal expression.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","matching.counterparties"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E001"],"rationale":"The aggregator node is linked to the counterparty-matching node by the frozen rule edge for the aggregator's responsibility to match P2P transactions."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","node_ids":["matching.counterparties","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R005-E002"],"rationale":"The qualitative counterparty-switching condition is represented by the frozen conditional_on edge from counterparty matching to local pricing; no additional variable or parameter nodes are declared because the source supplies no formal matching variables or fixed parameters in this scope."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
